<Base title="How Stateside researches, reviews and publishes"><article class="container"><div class="page-intro"><span class="eyebrow">THE EDITORIAL STANDARD</span><h1>Useful answers.<br/>Visible boundaries.</h1><p class="lede">We publish preparation material for owners and managers taking an established business model into the U.S. Every answer must explain the decision it helps you make.</p></div><div class="article-body" style="max-width:800px"><h2>Audience and scope</h2><p>The primary reader owns a business whose managers already handle day-to-day operations. Management independence is a question to investigate: sales, recruitment, technical decisions and key relationships may still depend on the owner. This is not a franchise-buyer or investor-visa directory.</p><p>Coverage includes federal, all 50 state and D.C. records; a separate territorial workstream; European and Southeast Asian origin records; and named local markets. The <a href="/coverage/">coverage register</a> distinguishes topic-specific evidence from work not yet researched.</p><h2>How a guide earns publication</h2><ol><li>Identify one distinct reader question and the decision it changes.</li><li>Read primary legislation, regulations, official instructions, forms or relevant court documents. Search snippets are discovery aids.</li><li>Record applicability, source passages, dates and conflicting evidence. Separate a legal requirement from a contract condition or business practice.</li><li>Write an original explanation, realistic example and useful evidence checklist.</li><li>Review its sources, scope and calculations. Keep material unresolved issues explicit or hold the page out of publication.</li></ol><p>This initial batch received AI-assisted source research and editorial review. No legal professional has reviewed or endorsed it. No author qualifications, transaction outcomes or customer testimonials are implied.</p><h2>What our statuses mean</h2><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Status</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Verified</td><td>The recorded general proposition was checked against its cited authority in this review. It has a stated scope.</td></tr><tr><td>Conditional</td><td>A supported rule depends on facts, exceptions or a separate analysis.</td></tr><tr><td>Unresolved</td><td>An access problem, conflict or judgment question remains material.</td></tr><tr><td>Not researched</td><td>There is no substantive finding. This is never “no requirement.”</td></tr><tr><td>Not applicable</td><td>A reason must be recorded; absence of information is insufficient.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h2>Dates, revisions and change detection</h2><p>A fetch date records access. A substantive verification date records the review of the source against the summary. A modification date records an edit. They are not interchangeable.</p><p>The project includes a daily source-check workflow that compares normalized source content with a retained baseline and creates a review report. The schedule becomes active only after the workflow is pushed to a GitHub default branch with Actions enabled. HTTP errors and detected changes identify affected pages and tools; they never automatically publish a new legal interpretation.</p><p>Volatile thresholds, filing fees, tariffs, immigration rules, employment rules and litigation are priority review topics. A review interval is an editorial policy, not a guarantee that the law remains unchanged. Git history preserves reviewed versions. CLI review gates and dependency reports support the next publication batch.</p><h2 id="geography">Geographic convention</h2><p>The origin register uses the United Nations M49 Europe grouping as a geographic baseline, including EU and non-EU economies. Supplemental records separately identify transcontinental countries and Kosovo, and dependent areas are distinguished from sovereign countries. This is a research convention, not a position on sovereignty or treaty eligibility.</p><p>Southeast Asia includes Brunei, Cambodia, Indonesia, Laos, Malaysia, Myanmar, the Philippines, Singapore, Thailand, Timor-Leste and Vietnam. All are registered even where detailed research is pending.</p><p>A commercial region or metro is a comparison area. It is not itself a permit authority. Municipal boundaries, counties, special districts, an exact address and the regulated activity can all change the operating workstream.</p><h2>Learning and evidence are separate</h2><p>Reading a guide is learning progress. Recording a document is evidence progress. The responsible person must confirm what the evidence establishes. We do not display compliance percentages, exemption approvals or probabilities of success.</p><h2>Search and indexing</h2><p>Public guides use static HTML, descriptive titles, canonical URLs, source links and a sitemap containing published pages. Candidate pages do not generate indexed placeholders. Private plan data never appears in public URLs or rendered HTML.</p><p>We follow <a href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/fundamentals/ai-optimization-guide">Google’s current generative-search guidance</a> and <a href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/essentials/spam-policies">spam policies</a>: useful original content and normal search fundamentals. No special schema, sitemap or AI file guarantees indexing, citations or rankings. No search volume or conversion estimates have been invented.</p><h2>Corrections and the next review</h2><p>The source repository contains a correction and review queue. Maintainers should record the disputed claim, relevant authority, affected page and proposed correction; retain the prior version; and complete substantive review before publishing. The site publisher must designate a public corrections contact before a wider release.</p></div></article></Base>
